---
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
const title = "AI-Assisted Dafny Code Generation and Verification";
const rows = [
  ["Bounded Counter", "1", "3"],
  ["Authorized Access", "1", "1"],
  ["Account Transfer", "3", "1"],
];
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} — Bilal Amin`}
  description="A small research prototype comparing two prompts for Dafny generation with verifier-guided repair across three scenarios."
>
  <article class="case-study py-10 text-white sm:py-16">
    <a
      href="/#projects"
      class="text-sm text-neutral underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-white"
      >← All projects</a
    >
    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-neutral">Research prototype · 2026</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 font-serif text-4xl leading-tight sm:text-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-neutral">
      Can an AI agent turn a short English requirement into Dafny code that
      passes formal verification? I built a small Python pipeline to explore
      prompt design and verifier-guided repair, then compared two prompt
      profiles on three scenarios.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-5 text-sm">
      <a
        class="underline underline-offset-4"
        href="https://github.com/Astavicious/Entry-Task"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer">Source and experiment artifacts ↗</a
      >
      <a
        class="underline underline-offset-4"
        href="https://github.com/Astavicious/Entry-Task/blob/main/results/benchmark/20260831T163605Z_summary.json"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer">Saved results ↗</a
      >
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Generated code can look plausible while violating a requirement. Dafny
        checks an implementation against written preconditions, postconditions,
        and invariants. The experiment therefore separated two questions:
        whether the code verifies, and whether its specification captures the
        original English requirement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Architecture and approach</h2>
      <p>
        Python coordinates the Codex SDK and Dafny CLI. The implementation has
        separate modules for prompts, model calls and code extraction,
        verification, the repair loop, and benchmarking. Each model call runs in
        a fresh temporary directory outside the repository to avoid hidden
        repository context.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-6">
        <a
          href="/dafny-pipeline.png"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
          aria-label="Open full-size pipeline slide"
          ><img
            src="/dafny-pipeline.png"
            width="1920"
            height="1080"
            alt="Pipeline: requirement, general or improved prompt, Codex response, unchanged Dafny source, verifier; failures return the requirement, previous source, and complete diagnostics for repair."
            class="w-full rounded-xl"
          /></a
        >
        <figcaption>
          Pipeline slide from my project presentation. Open the image to inspect
          it at full size.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Read a scenario requirement and assemble the general or
          verification-aware (“improved”) prompt.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask Codex to generate Dafny; save the raw response and extracted
          source without manually editing the program.
        </li>
        <li>Run <code>dafny verify</code> and save its output.</li>
        <li>
          On failure, send the original requirement, previous source, and
          complete verifier feedback back to the model.
        </li>
        <li>
          Stop at a verification success or after three total attempts: one
          generation and at most two repairs.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The improved prompt adds guidance on meaningful contracts, requirement
        preservation, and avoiding proof shortcuts. Its name describes the
        intervention; it does not imply better performance.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm">
        Technologies: Python · Codex SDK · gpt-5.4-mini · Dafny 4.11 ·
        subprocess · JSON experiment reports
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Three evaluation scenarios</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Bounded Counter:</strong> increment and reset a counter while preserving
          non-negativity and a configurable maximum; an increment at the limit leaves
          the value unchanged.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Authorized Access:</strong> grant access only to authorized users
          and update authorization state correctly when granting or revoking it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Account Transfer:</strong> constrain the amount to a non-negative
          value within the source balance, keep both balances non-negative, and preserve
          their combined balance.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Saved experiment results</h2>
      <p>
        The clean batch dated August 31, 2026 used three scenarios × two prompt
        profiles × one model × one repetition: six runs. The table reports the
        successful attempt number from those saved runs, rather than a new
        generation benchmark.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="mb-3 text-left text-neutral"
            >Successful attempt, with a maximum of three attempts per run</caption
          >
          <thead
            ><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>General</th><th>Improved</th></tr></thead
          >
          <tbody
            >{
              rows.map(([scenario, general, improved]) => (
                <tr>
                  <>
                    <th scope="row">{scenario}</th>
                    <td>{general}</td>
                    <td>{improved}</td>
                  </>
                </tr>
              ))
            }</tbody
          >
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Four of six programs verified on the first attempt. Both profiles
        reached 2/3 first-attempt successes and 3/3 successes within the attempt
        limit, using five total attempts each. The feedback loop repaired both
        initial failures. All six final programs were warning-free in the saved
        results.
      </p>
      <p>
        I separately reviewed the final programs against the supplied
        requirements and recorded coverage under reasonable interpretations.
        This was a human assessment, not an automated proof that the English
        meaning was captured.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A repair example</h2>
      <p>
        The improved bounded-counter run first generated unsupported class-level
        invariant syntax. The second attempt introduced a <code>Valid()</code> predicate,
        but its increment method did not require a valid starting state. On attempt
        three, the model added <code>requires Valid()</code>; Dafny then
        reported six verified obligations and zero errors.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-6">
        <a
          href="/dafny-repair.png"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
          aria-label="Open full-size repair slide"
          ><img
            src="/dafny-repair.png"
            width="1920"
            height="1080"
            alt="Attempt two could not prove the Valid postcondition. Attempt three adds requires Valid() to Increment and verifies with six verified obligations and zero errors."
            class="w-full rounded-xl"
            loading="lazy"
          /></a
        >
        <figcaption>Repair slide from my project presentation.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The verifier supplied a failed proof obligation; the model made the
        repair. This illustrates why initial-state assumptions must be explicit
        in contracts.
      </p>
      <p>The successful method contract, as text:</p>
      <pre
        class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-4 text-sm"><code>{`method Increment()
  requires Valid()
  modifies this
  ensures Valid()
  ensures max == old(max)
  ensures value == if old(value) < old(max)
    then old(value) + 1 else old(value)`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Limitations and lessons</h2>
      <p>
        The prompt profiles tied in one small experiment. One model, one
        repetition, and three short scenarios cannot establish that either
        prompt is generally better or that the profiles are equivalent. Model
        output is stochastic, and harder programs may expose problems with
        loops, framing, and termination.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strongest lesson was the usefulness of concrete verifier feedback.
        Experimental isolation also mattered, and verification success still
        needed a separate review of specification faithfulness.
      </p>
      <p>
        A next experiment would use at least five repetitions, harder tasks, and
        a more systematic semantic review. Those are proposed extensions, not
        completed features.
      </p>
    </section>
    <a
      href="/#projects"
      class="inline-block py-8 text-sm underline underline-offset-4"
      >← Back to projects</a
    >
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .case-study section {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
  .case-study h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .case-study section p {
    color: var(--color-neutral);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .case-study ul,
  .case-study ol {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral);
  }
  .case-study ul {
    list-style: disc;
  }
  .case-study ol {
    list-style: decimal;
  }
  .case-study li {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  .case-study strong {
    color: white;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .case-study code {
    color: white;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .case-study th,
  .case-study td {
    padding: 0.85rem 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ffffff26;
  }
  .case-study tbody th {
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .case-study figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral);
  }
</style>
